.TH CHTTP_RUN_QUERY 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_run_query \- run one request with explicit headers, with the default client
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttp_run_query(chttp_method_t method, const char *url,
                              const chttp_request_body_t *body,
                              chmap headers,
                              chttpcli_response **resp_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This is a convenience wrapper that builds a chttp_request_t
internally, attaches headers to it without taking ownership, calls
chttp_do and then frees the request it built. Of the convenience
functions for the default client, this is the only one that takes a
header set of your choice in a single call. With chttp_get, chttp_post
and the others you have to build a chttp_request_t yourself, with
chttp_request_create followed by one chttp_request_set_header call per
header.

The caller keeps full ownership of headers. The function only borrows
the map for the duration of the call, and never frees or changes it.

The keys in headers can be in any case, such as "Host", "Content-Type"
or "Accept". The module adds its own default value for some keys
(host, accept, user-agent, content-length, content-type, authorization
and expect) and looks each of them up in headers without regard to
case, so one of your headers in its natural case replaces the
module's default for it, and the two never both go onto the wire.

A chmap compares keys by their exact bytes, so headers can hold two
keys that differ as bytes but name the same header under HTTP's
case-insensitive rules, such as "Host" and "host" together.
chttp_request_set_header lowercases every key before storing it, so it
can never create that situation, but a headers map you build yourself
gives no such guarantee. Only one key of such a pair reaches the wire,
never both: the key that went into headers last wins, the same as an
update of one exact key. The client drops the other one instead of
sending it as a second header line.

Because you pass headers directly, they skip the validation of
chttp_request_set_header. Every key must be a token, and no value may
hold a byte below 0x20 other than a horizontal tab (CR and LF among
them), or 0x7F. Those are the rules of chttp_request_set_header, for
the same reason: data with a CRLF in it could otherwise inject extra
header lines or split the request in two. For such a headers map the
function returns ccol_invalid_args before it opens any connection.
headers must not hold a "Transfer-Encoding" key either, in any case,
for the reason chttp_request_set_header rejects that name: this client
never applies a transfer coding to a request body, so it cannot honor
such a header, and on the wire it would sit next to the module's
automatic Content-Length over a body with no transfer coding, which is
ambiguous framing.

The function DOES accept a "Content-Length" key in headers, but checks
it on a request that carries a body (POST, PUT or PATCH). A valid
value is a plain unsigned string of decimal digits, with no
leading '+' or '-' and no whitespace in it, and it must also match the
real length of the body that the client sends. A bad value gives
ccol_invalid_args once the request reaches the wire. chttpclient_do(3)
and chttp_request_set_header(3) describe the same check, which exists
for the same reason: the declared framing and the wire must not
disagree.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
method     The HTTP method: one of CHTTP_GET to CHTTP_OPTIONS.
           CHTTP_ANY and values outside the enum are refused.
url        The target URL. It must not be NULL.
body       The request body, or NULL for a request with no body.
headers    A chmap(char* -> char*) of the request headers, or NULL for
           no headers. The function borrows it for the duration of
           the call and neither consumes nor frees it. Every key must
           be a token, no value may hold a byte below 0x20 other than
           a horizontal tab, or 0x7F, and no key may be
           "Transfer-Encoding".
resp_out   It must not be NULL. The function sets it to NULL first,
           and stores the pointer to the response there if the call
           succeeds.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The same codes as chttpclient_do. It also returns
ccol_not_enough_memory when the internal request object cannot be
allocated, and ccol_invalid_args when url or resp_out is NULL, when
method names no HTTP method (CHTTP_ANY included), when body has a NULL
data with a len that is not zero, when headers holds a key that is not
a token or a value with a control byte other than a horizontal tab
(CR and LF among them) or 0x7F, or when headers holds a
"Transfer-Encoding" key.
.fi
